 The browser holds the laid-out widget tree so a page link can be hit-tested
 against bounds that exist. This measures the thing that makes keeping it
 safe: a click cycle must leave the heap exactly where it found it.

 It matters because the pane loop never returns, and a bump allocator
 reclaims only by returning or by an explicit restore. Anything a cycle
 allocates and does not release is permanent. `apps/browser/opening.codex`
 already ran out of memory once for that reason.

 The discipline being measured is the ORDER. A cycle releases to
 `bs-laid-mark` BEFORE it dispatches, never after: the tree is the tip of the
 heap, the new state is allocated above where it stood, and a release that
 came second would have to free memory underneath state that must survive.
 `browser-click-code` answers an Integer precisely so the hit can be read out
 of the tree while it is alive and still mean something once it is gone.

 The rows report a DELTA per cycle and not a frontier, because a frontier is
 a count and counts in this tree have all been wrong. Zero is the claim.

 y=200 at x=200 is page background: it is above the first link row, which
 `browser-click-links` records at 240. That is the case that has to be free,
 because it is every click that lands on nothing.

Section: Test

  cycle : BrowserState -> BrowserState
  cycle (st) =
    let code = browser-click-code st 200 200
    in let released = __heap-restore (st.bs-laid-mark)
    in let next = browser-apply-click st 200 200 code
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in let laid = widget-layout (browser-render next) (next.bs-theme) (layout-rect 0 0 1024 768)
    in browser-memo-laid next laid mark

  spin : BrowserState, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (st) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then __heap-save
    else spin (cycle st) (i + 1) n

  from-fresh : Integer -> Integer
  from-fresh (n) =
    let st = browser-init 1024 768 browser-theme-default display-init-text-fallback
    in let mark = __heap-save
    in let laid = widget-layout (browser-render st) (st.bs-theme) (layout-rect 0 0 1024 768)
    in spin (browser-memo-laid st laid mark) 0 n

 Each measurement releases to its own base, so the second starts from the
 same frontier as the first and the subtraction is not measuring the first
 run's residue.

  measure : Integer -> Integer
  measure (n) =
    let base = __heap-save
    in let top = from-fresh n
    in let released = __heap-restore base
    in top - base

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let one = measure 1
    in let many = measure 51
    in act
      print-line-uni ("per-cycle=" & integer-to-text ((many - one) / 50))
    end
